{
  // ── Second type-check pass: the untyped half of the codebase ──────────────
  //
  // `jsconfig.json` runs `strict: true` but `checkJs: false`, so its green "0
  // errors" only ever covered files with a typed script block. Everything on
  // plain `<script>` — including +page.svelte, CockpitShell and AgentWorkspace,
  // ~16.6 kLOC of the Terminal IA surface — was never looked at.
  //
  // That blind spot shipped real defects, all of the same shape and all of them
  // caught by this config: `/compare` writing to the undefined return of
  // `addMsg`, `skillId` reading `.id` off a type whose id lives under `.meta`,
  // an `atts` mirror type still describing its pre-v1.8.1 shape, `esc` used
  // ~2000 lines above its declaration.
  //
  // WHY `strict: false` HERE. With the project's `strict: true` this pass
  // reports 2 498 errors; with it off, 217. The 2 281-error difference is
  // almost entirely `noImplicitAny` — unannotated callback parameters — and not
  // one of the defects above needed it. This config buys the bugs at a tenth of
  // the cost. The `.ts` files keep full strictness from `jsconfig.json`; this is
  // an ADDITIONAL pass, not a replacement, and relaxing a rule never turns a
  // passing file red.
  //
  // Reached 0 on 2026-08-02 and is a CI gate from that moment
  // (.github/workflows/ci.yml, "svelte-check --checkJs"). It is not merged into
  // jsconfig.json and will not be: the two configs differ on TWO axes, and
  // every single-config combination is worse. `strict: true` + `checkJs: true`
  // is the ~2 500-error wall above; `strict: false` + `checkJs: true` would
  // drop real coverage on the .ts half. Two passes with different trade-offs
  // is the end state.
  "extends": "./jsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "checkJs": true,
    "strict": false
  }
}
